; Example mod.ini for a Texture Toolkit texture mod.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Make a folder for your mod inside TT, next to dump and inject, for example:
;
;          <game folder>\TT\DualShock\
;
;   2. Put your replacement .dds files in it, each named after the hash of the texture it
;      replaces (5D3E2CCE1A7740B2.dds, 0x5D3E2CCE1A7740B2.dds, or Special K's 5D3E2CCE.dds).
;      Subfolders are fine, so you can organise them however you like:
;
;          TT\DualShock\
;              mod.ini
;              buttons\5D3E2CCE1A7740B2.dds
;              menus\0A1B2C3D4E5F6071.dds
;
;   3. Copy this file into the folder and rename it to mod.ini (exactly that name), then
;      fill in the values below. The file is optional: without it the mod still loads, and
;      the panel shows the folder name instead.
;
;   4. Press "Reload replacements" on the panel's Mod files page, or restart the game. The
;      mod appears there with a switch, and arrows to move it up or down the load order.
;      Where two mods replace the same texture, the one higher in the list wins. A new mod
;      goes in at the top, so it applies in full as soon as it is installed.
;
; To share the mod, zip the whole folder. Players unzip it into their TT folder.
;
; Every key is optional. Lines starting with ; are comments. Save the file as plain text;
; for letters outside English, save it as "UTF-16 LE" (Notepad: Save As > Encoding).

[Mod]
; Shown as the mod's title in the panel. Defaults to the folder name.
Name=DualShock Button Prompts

; Shown next to the title as "by ...".
Author=Your Name

; Shown next to the title as "v...". Any text works: 1.0, 2.3b, 2026-10-04.
Version=1.0

; One line shown under the title. Keep it short; it wraps in the panel.
Description=Replaces the keyboard prompts with PlayStation buttons.

; Whether the mod is switched on when someone first installs it: 1 or 0, true or false,
; yes or no, on or off. Defaults to on. Players can still switch it from the panel, and
; their choice (saved in TextureToolkit.ini under [ModEnabled]) always wins over this.
Enabled=1
